        "body": "If the exception comes from a system outage, record the workaround, the temporary identifier, and the reconciliation owner. If the owner asks for more evidence, record the request, the due time, and the person responsible for obtaining it. A stop-work rule should name the trigger, the person who can release the work, and the evidence needed to restart. The escalation packet should be short enough to read on a phone and complete enough to decide without a meeting.\n\nClassify exceptions by whether the work can continue, must pause, or needs an immediate owner decision. The packet should state what the operator did not do and why, so the owner can see the boundary that was preserved. When the request conflicts with policy or the approved scope, the operator should route it rather than negotiate a workaround in the queue. The escalation packet should be short enough to read on a phone and complete enough to decide without a meeting.\n\nTrack repeated exceptions separately from one-off errors; a pattern may point to a form, a queue rule, or a permissions problem. An escalation packet should include the outside counsel invoice link, verified facts, the unresolved decision, the deadline, actions already taken, and the safest next step. If the same stop trigger appears repeatedly, change the intake rule or the deadline entry requirement instead of coaching around it. Do not close an exception because the customer stopped replying; record the last contact and the next attempt."
